# Strings and branding

## Lingui

- Every user-visible string goes through Lingui: `<Trans>…</Trans>` in JSX, `` t`…` `` from
  `useLingui()` / `@lingui/core/macro` in code. No bare English in JSX.
- Do not concatenate translated fragments. Use placeholders: `<Trans>Hello {name}</Trans>`.
- After adding or changing strings: `pnpm --filter client exec lingui extract` then
  `lingui compile --typescript`, and commit the updated `catalogs/en/messages.po`.
- Other languages' catalogs are upstream translations. Leave them alone unless the task is
  about them. A new message falls back to English there; that is fine.
- `lingui extract` leaves obsolete entries (`#~`). They are not shown to users; do not hand-edit
  catalogs to remove them.

## Branding: what the user sees

- The product is **AMIXOF**. User-visible text, titles, `alt`, `aria-label`, `<title>`, manifest,
  emails links, and the wordmark must not say "Stoat" or "Revolt".
- The wordmark is `AmixofWordmark` from `@revolt/ui`. Do not reintroduce
  `wordmark.svg?component-solid`.
- Links that used to go to Stoat donate, feedback, changelog, developer, social, Lounge, or
  status pages must go to AMIXOF equivalents or be removed.
- `/source` and the in-app Source link must keep pointing at the public GitHub repo (AGPL §13).

## Branding: what stays as is

- Code and protocol names: `stoat.js`, `isStoat`, `STOAT_HOST`, `StoatOrigin`,
  `/.well-known/stoat`, `Revolt.toml`, `@revolt/*` aliases, CSS variable names, package names.
- Upstream copyright notices and the `LICENSE`.
- Code inside `instance.isStoat` branches (never rendered here) and `AndroidNag` (only runs on
  `stoat.chat` / `localhost`).

## Check

`node .cursor/skills/amixof-audit/scripts/brand-scan.mjs` lists user-facing Stoat/Revolt strings
that are still reachable from source. It must report zero new findings before you ship.
